WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.400
let's try a practice problem regarding
enthalpy of vaporization. so the question

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:19.289
is how much heat is required to vaporize
1.500 liters of water at 37 degrees

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:24.630
Celsius so in order to answer this we
need to know that the density of water

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:30.000
at 37 degrees Celsius is actually just a
hair under one gram per milliliter which

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.260
is what we're used to we're going to use
point nine nine three four grams per

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:40.770
milliliter to be very specific and then
the heat of vaporization of water at 37

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:47.160
degrees Celsius is forty three point
four six kilojoules per mole so this is

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.280
all the data you need to answer this
question go ahead and give it a try.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.560
let's take our one point five zero zero
liters of water and we're gonna want to

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.150
do some stoichiometric calculations so
we've got to get two moles our goal is

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:22.439
always going to be to get two moles
somehow with these things so let's take

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:26.909
our one point five zero zero liters and
we want milliliters because our density

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:31.619
was grams per milliliter so let's
multiply by a thousand milliliters over

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:37.439
one liter and we're gonna get 1,500
milliliters now that we have milliliters

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:42.689
we can use our density 1500 milliliters
times zero point nine nine three four

00:01:42.689 --> 00:01:48.240
grams per milliliter and that is going
to give us a mass of fourteen ninety

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.329
grams so that is the mass of water
contained in that volume of water

00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:57.210
specifically at this temperature 37
degrees Celsius right if we had a

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.079
different temperature things might be
slightly different then we take our

00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:05.880
fourteen ninety grams of water and we're
gonna multiply by one mol / eighteen

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.440
point oh two grams that's the molar mass
of water and we know we therefore have

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:15.880
eighty two point six nine moles of water
that's how many moles of water are in

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.370
this volume of water at this temperature
then lastly we can just use the given

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:26.440
thermo chemical data we know that eighty
two point six nine moles times forty

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:31.810
three point four six kilojoules of
energy required per mole for

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:36.010
vaporization to occur that's going to
give us a total of three thousand five

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:43.020
hundred ninety four kilojoules of energy required to vaporize this volume of water at this temperature.

